'''Christianity in Guajicutea''' is the island's largest Christian tradition. Most Guajicutean Christians belong to [[Petran Catholicism]], a Sartan-linked Catholic tradition that became dominant through colonial administration, schooling, and urban settlement. Smaller communities follow [[Burawi Catholic Church|Burawi Catholic]] practice, Protestant denominations, or [[Catharism]] traditions, especially among Bayani families descended from migrants from Balisca.

As of 2025, the Christian population is divided into Petran Catholics (86% of Christians), Burawi Catholics (7%), Protestants (4%), Cathars (2%), and uncategorized or unknown Christians (1%). Petran Catholicism is strongest in the main towns and in families tied to the Sartan-speaking civic elite. Burawi Catholic communities are concentrated around Waribá and among Guaji families in nearby districts, where older Burawi church networks remained visible after the colonial period. Protestants are mostly recent immigrants and their descendants. Cathar communities are a small minority, rooted largely in Bayani families that arrived from Balisca in later migration waves and in some cases during the Baliscan colonial period, with urban and intermarried household networks.

== History ==

Christianity entered Guajicutea during the colonial period. Burawi missionaries established the first durable church institutions after contact, while the later Petran Catholic Church expanded through Sartan influence, administration, and trade. In the early colonial period, Guaji converts often blended Christian worship with local burial practice, sacred landscape, and older ritual memory rather than abandoning them entirely.

The 1554 settlement after the First Guaji Civil War gave Petran Catholicism a firmer foothold in south and west Guajicutea, while the Waribá corridor remained more closely tied to Burawi Catholic clergy and institutions. In later centuries, Protestant congregations arrived through immigration and commercial contact, especially from communities with stronger non-Catholic traditions. Cathar communities remained a small minority, but they became a recognizable part of Guajicutean Christianity through urban settlement, intermarriage, and Bayani immigration from Balisca during both colonial and later periods.

