== Petran Catcholicism ==

Petran Catholics form the largest and most visible Christian community in Guajicutea. They are concentrated in Waribá, Tabakurí, and the larger coastal towns, especially in neighborhoods with older Sartan-speaking families, civil-service households, and long-established trading families. Most parish life revolves around Sunday mass, feast days, baptisms, weddings, funerals, and catechism. In the larger towns, parish churches also function as neighborhood gathering places, schools, and centers of charity and mutual aid; in smaller districts, the same role is often filled by mission chapels or compact parish churches.

Compared with Petran Catholics in mainland Sarta, Guajicutean Petran Catholics tend to be more bilingual and more locally adapted. Formal liturgy is often in Sartan in the cities, but Guaji is commonly heard in household devotion, rural parishes, and informal prayer. The island's Petran Catholic practice is also shaped by older Guaji habits around kinship, memory, and death: families may keep home altars, sponsor memorial meals, and treat parish life as part of a wider household calendar rather than as a separate clerical sphere. The doctrine is the same as mainland Petran Catholicism, but the style is less rigid and more visibly island-based.

In daily life, Petran Catholic observance is usually quiet and routine. Many families begin the day with a short prayer, attend work or school, and return to church life for evening devotions, rosary, or community meetings when needed. Children commonly pass through catechism and first communion, while adults turn to the parish for weddings, funerals, and practical support. The religion is therefore both a formal church tradition and a social rhythm that structures family life, holidays, and public ceremony.

