== Burawi Catholicism ==

Burawi Catholics make up the second-largest Christian community in Guajicutea. They are concentrated in the districts around Waribá, especially among Guaji families with older Burawi ties, as well as in a few mixed urban neighborhoods where Burawi church institutions remained active after the colonial period. Burawi Catholic congregations are in communion with the Petran church, but they keep their own hierarchy and look to the [[Cardinal of Burawa]] as their highest church authority.

Compared with the Petran church, Burawi Catholicism is generally seen as less rigid in doctrine and more willing to adapt to local customs. In Guajicutea this has long shown up in the churches themselves: Burawi cathedrals and parish churches use more pre-Christian Guaji symbolism in decoration, more local imagery in carved stone and painted panels, and a less severe visual style than the Petran churches. Burawi clergy have also tended to be more comfortable with Guaji language in worship and with older household customs in parish life.

Cremation was one of the clearest historical differences between the two Catholic traditions. Burawi clergy in Guajicutea tolerated it earlier and more openly than Petran clergy did, and that made Burawi Catholicism feel closer to Guaji family practice. In modern Guajicutea, Catholic teaching no longer treats cremation as a major dividing line, but the older distinction still shapes family memory and church identity.

Daily life for Burawi Catholics is similar to that of other Catholics: Sunday mass, feast days, baptisms, weddings, funerals, catechism, and parish charity. The difference is mostly in tone and setting. Burawi families are more likely to attend churches that feel locally Guaji in language and design, and parish events often feel less formal than in Petran Catholic neighborhoods. In practice, Burawi Catholicism sits between the mainline Petran style and older Guaji religious custom, rather than standing completely apart from either.

